#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180406} :activity/id #uuid "fa4e78d6-f3b0-503b-87cd-d288efaece3d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050639 :topic/id #uuid "5b3a4c91-51d4-5634-a8c3-82457409b255" :topic/math-academy-id 3741} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180406 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180685 :tutorial/content "The range of a radical function $f(x)=\\sqrt[n]{x}$ depends on whether the index $n$ of the radical is even or odd. \n\nLet's start with the odd case first. What is the range of \n\n$$\nf(x) = -2\\sqrt[3]{3x-2}+2?\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the range of $y = \\sqrt[3] x$ is\n$y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTransformations do not change this interval. Therefore, the range of $f(x)$ is $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6b9dccc8-5bda-5458-a62d-5d74d82eb634" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4863 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "70a49b8b-925c-5999-90d0-2b2d817e962c" :step/math-academy-id 30458 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180410}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180407 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179684 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179543 :question/id #uuid "7096f867-7d37-5630-a885-d0a4f4a6acb7" :question/math-academy-id "e-8142"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52e02202-ebdf-5987-bbaf-b5eb204c3892" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050642}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567080}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Radical Function With an Even Index"} :step/id #uuid "d6b1dfbb-7d27-561e-82ff-dafcb19e06f5" :step/math-academy-id 30459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180410 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179687 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179546 :question/id #uuid "9af3bd0c-c209-508f-b806-bb9426f2c0da" :question/math-academy-id "e-8719"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3abc2f98-9bbd-5a33-87fc-f9459fb18c60" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049528}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567090}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Radical Function With an Odd Index"} :step/id #uuid "f116d4fa-4115-5e01-a07f-ef9053e45179" :step/math-academy-id 32415 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180413}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180413 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180686 :tutorial/content "Let's now focus on the even case. What is the range of \n\n$$\ng(x) = -3\\sqrt[4]{2x-1}-2?\n$$\n\nThe range of $\\sqrt[4] x$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4] x \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply transformations to the inequality above to turn the left-hand side into $g(x).$\n\nFirst, remember that horizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{2x-1} \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-3$ and then subtracting $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[4]{2x-1} & \\ge 0 \\\\ -3\\sqrt[4]{2x-1} & \\le 0 \\\\ -3\\sqrt[4]{2x-1}-2 & \\le -2 \\\\ g(x) & \\le -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $g(x) \\in (-\\infty,-2].$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=g(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b7f3dc07cb72ea087d7cc4ec01379efa212b89fb8ab397b6df5bccfe75e66f5.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "a811e71d-0dee-5896-8be1-74b83f779ac4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5199 :tutorial/title "The Range of a Transformed Radical Function With an Even Index"} :step/id #uuid "224fb34f-79c0-5dc1-b4cd-378542ebe04b" :step/math-academy-id 32637 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180407}}] :activity/title "The Range of a Transformed Radical Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179888}]}
